## {% if serverData.envVars.length %}4{% else %}3{% endif %}. Register the server in this project

For Claude Code, do this command in the root directory of the project:

```
claude mcp add {{ serverData.id }} --scope project{% if serverData.envVars.length %}{% for envVar in serverData.envVars %} --env {{ envVar | safe }}={{ "${" }}{{ envVar | safe }}{{ "}" }}{% endfor %}{% endif %} -- dotnet run ./.mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }} -v q
```

The `--scope project` option writes the `.mcp.json` file in the root directory of the project.
Commit that file. Then each person who clones the project gets the server. The `--` separator is
necessary, because the command that comes after it contains the `-v q` option.
{% if not serverData.envVars.length %}
The command writes an empty `"env": {}` block. The JSON example below does not show that block. The
two forms are the same. Do not change the file.
{%- endif %}

Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, and Windsurf read a JSON configuration file, and they use the
`mcpServers` key. Put this text in the `.mcp.json` file in the root directory of the project:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{ serverData.id }}": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "dotnet",
      "args": ["run", "./.mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }}", "-v", "q"]{% if serverData.envVars.length %},
      "env": {
{%- for envVar in serverData.envVars %}
        "{{ envVar | safe }}": "{{ "${" }}{{ envVar | safe }}{{ "}" }}"{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
      }{% endif %}
    }
  }
}
```

Visual Studio and VS Code use `servers` in place of `mcpServers`. The object in the key is the same.

The path is relative to the project. It stays correct for each person who clones the project, and on
each operating system. The client finds the path from its working directory, which is the project
directory. A client that starts a server from a different directory needs an absolute path.
{%- if serverData.envVars.length %}

Claude Code replaces `${...}` with the value from the environment when it reads the `.mcp.json`
file. The file thus contains the name of the variable and no secret. A client that does not replace
a variable needs the value in the file. Keep such a configuration out of version control.
{%- endif %}

## {% if serverData.envVars.length %}5{% else %}4{% endif %}. Check the installation

```
claude mcp list
claude mcp get {{ serverData.id }}
```

**If an agent does the installation, stop at the `Pending approval` state.** Claude Code connects a
project server only after a person approves the server. An agent cannot approve the server for the
user, and an agent cannot start again the client that contains it. Thus
`Status: ⏸ Pending approval (run claude to approve)` is the correct result. It is not a failure. Do
not do the command again. Stop here, and tell the user to start Claude Code again and approve the
prompt, or to use the `/mcp` command.

**Do not test the server manually.** If you send JSON-RPC lines in one block, the server gives no
output. Standard input comes to the end of the file before the host answers `initialize`, and the
log shows only `transport completed reading messages`. This looks like a defective server, but the
server is correct. The first client start is the test.

After a person starts the client again in the project directory and approves the server,
`{{ serverData.id }}` shows the status `connected`, with {{ serverData.tools.length }} tool(s). Then
tell the model to call `{{ serverData.tools[0].name | wireName | safe }}`, and make sure that a
result comes back.

